On 1/21/2010 3:25 PM, Ssulian wrote:
> Is it a common strategy to engineer bones levels?  For example, one
> could keep a large cache of items in a box on each level as one
> descended, and move this stash as each level progresses.  One could
> put tins of good monsters to eat in this box too for a quick way to
> gain intrinsics for the person who finds the bones.  Also, I don't
> really know the specifics, but I believe there are certain factors
> that make a level more likely to be retained and served as a bones
> level.  These factors could be maximized, and even the character may
> intentionally die to set the bones level good.  Is this a common
> strategy?

Intentionally dying to create a good bones file seems like
a pretty bad strategy to me.  First of all, you are never
guaranteed to leave one behind.  Even if you do it can
only be used by one future character.  And that future
character would be worse off than the one you're killing
to get the bones file since most of the stuff would now be
cursed.  I can't see any advantage to it.

